<!--
  Derby Bins — sitemap.

  Hand-written. There is no generator and no build step on this site, so this
  file is the single place the indexable URL list exists. Keep it by hand.

  Three URLs, and they are exactly the pages that ask to be indexed:

    /          index, follow   (index.html)
    /sources   index, follow   (sources.html)
    /support   no robots meta, so indexable by default (support.html)

  Two pages are deliberately absent:

    /privacy   carries <meta name="robots" content="noindex">. Listing a
               noindex URL in a sitemap is a contradiction: it tells a crawler
               "index this page here" on the one page that says "do not index
               this". Google is documented as ignoring noindex pages found via
               a sitemap, so the cost is not a penalty — it is that the sitemap
               stops being a truthful statement of what should be indexed. That
               matters more than usual here, because this site exists so a Play
               reviewer can check that collection data comes from official
               council sources; a sitemap that misreports the index surface is
               the kind of small inaccuracy that invites a second look.

    /404       is served from hosting/public/404.html, which carries
               <meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow">, and is not a
               real address anyway.

  Paths use the CLEAN form (/sources, not /sources.html) because index.html and
  sources.html each declare exactly that URL in their <link rel="canonical">.
  support.html declares no canonical at all, deliberately — one there would only
  restate the clean /support URL firebase.json already serves — so /support is a
  choice rather than a restatement. In every case the .html URLs are rewritten
  to the same files by firebase.json and are the same documents, so listing them
  would be duplicate entries for no gain.

  No <lastmod>. There is no build step and no deploy timestamp available here,
  and a hand-written date nobody updates is worse than no date: crawlers treat a
  stale or wrong <lastmod> as a signal and may re-crawl on your behalf. <lastmod>
  is optional in the protocol; omitting it is valid.
-->
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
  <url>
    <loc>https://derbybins.web.app/</loc>
    <changefreq>monthly</changefreq>
    <priority>1.0</priority>
  </url>
  <url>
    <loc>https://derbybins.web.app/sources</loc>
    <changefreq>monthly</changefreq>
    <priority>0.8</priority>
  </url>
  <url>
    <loc>https://derbybins.web.app/support</loc>
    <changefreq>yearly</changefreq>
    <priority>0.5</priority>
  </url>
</urlset>